<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Essaouira</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mogador</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Essaouira, known to Europeans as Mogador, is a port city on the Atlantic coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammed-iii-of-morocco">Mohammed ben Abdallah</a> founded the present town in 1760 as a royal trading port, laid out by the French engineer Théodore Cornut within strong sea walls. It became Morocco's main port for European trade in the 19th century, and its medina is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/essaouira.jpg" alt="Essaouira" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Holger Uwe Schmitt · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%22Ein_bemerkenswertes_historisches_Denkmal%22._16.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Essaouira lies on the Atlantic coast of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, in a bay partly sheltered by the island of Mogador, which makes it one of the best anchorages on the Moroccan coast. Its Arabic name aṣ-Ṣwiṛa is the diminutive of a word meaning wall or rampart. Until the 1960s it was generally known by the Portuguese name Mogador, probably a corruption of Amegdul, a name mentioned by the 11th-century geographer al-Bakrī. The place has been occupied since prehistoric times.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Carthaginian navigator Hanno visited in the 5th century BCE and established the trading post of Arambys. Around the turn of the 1st century BCE and CE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-ii">Juba II of Mauretania</a> set up a factory making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrian-purple">Tyrian purple</a> dye from shells found on the rocks and nearby islands. In 1506 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-of-portugal">Manuel I of Portugal</a> ordered a fortress built, the Castelo Real de Mogador, which fell to the local Regraga fraternity in 1510. In 1629 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> tried to colonise Mogador under Admiral Isaac de Razilly, but the settlers withdrew after a few days.</p><p>The present city was built by the 'Alawid sultan Mohammed III, who wished to reorient his kingdom towards the Atlantic and to trade with European powers. For twelve years he directed the French engineer Théodore Cornut and other Moroccan and European architects and technicians. The harbour entrance, with the Porte de la Marine, was built by an English renegade known as Ahmed el Inglizi. Mohammed III closed the harbour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agadir">Agadir</a> in 1767 so that southern trade would pass through Essaouira, and he encouraged Moroccan Jews to settle and handle the European trade. Until the end of the 19th century Essaouira was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>'s principal port, receiving goods that came by caravan from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sub-saharan-africa">sub-Saharan Africa</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a>.</p><p>On 16 August 1844 the French Navy under the Prince de Joinville bombarded and briefly occupied Mogador in the First Franco-Moroccan War. From 1912 to 1956 the city was part of the French protectorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Essaouira has 82,962 inhabitants, according to the count of 2024. Its medina was designated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2001. The Jewish quarter, the mellah, contains many old synagogues, and the town has a large Jewish cemetery; in 1867 a visiting French botanist reported that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> made up half of the population of 12,000.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Essaouira” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sidi Mohammed ibn Abdallah founded <b>Essaouira</b> as a trading port and in 1777 was among the first rulers to recognise the United States.</blockquote><a href="/state/alaouite-sultanate">Alaouite Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
